    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)\\operatorname{LCM}(a,b)=ab$.\n\nFor each prime, the lower and higher exponents sum to the exponent in $ab$; unique prime factorisation gives the result.",
      "worked": "For any prime $p$ dividing $a$ or $b$, let its exponents in $a$ and $b$ be $m$ and $n$, using exponent $0$ when it is absent. The exponents in the GCD and LCM are respectively the lower and higher of $m$ and $n$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\min(m,n)+\\max(m,n)&=m+n.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus the exponent of every prime in $\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)\\operatorname{LCM}(a,b)$ equals its exponent in $ab$. By unique prime factorisation,\n\n$$\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)\\operatorname{LCM}(a,b)=ab.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
